+
+## High
+
+### H-1 — Health factor divides before multiplying: real max LTV is 50%, then a cliff
+
+**Where:** `LendingMarket.healthFactor` (L150).
+
+```solidity
+return (collateralValueUsd(user) / debtUsd) * liquidationThreshold * WAD / BPS;
+```
+
+Both values are 18-decimal USD amounts. So `collateral / debt` is a whole-number division that drops the fraction. That leaves the health factor only a few possible values: 0, 0.8, 1.6, 2.4, … (×1e18).
+
+**What happens to users:**
+- **Borrowing:** you need at least 2× collateral to borrow at all. A borrow at 55% LTV reverts **[PoC]**. The README's 80% threshold never applies.
+- **Liquidation:** borrow at exactly 50% LTV and a 0.5% price drop sends the health factor from 1.6 straight to 0.8. Anyone can liquidate, even though the correct health factor is still ~1.59 **[PoC: ETH $2000 → $1990 makes a 1 ETH / 1,000 USDC position liquidatable]**. The whole debt gets closed and the user loses the 5% bonus (~31% with H-2).
+- The UI or anyone reading the README will overstate how safe users are.
+
+**Fix:** Multiply first, divide once:
+
+```solidity
+return collateralValueUsd(user) * liquidationThreshold * WAD / (debtUsd * BPS);
+```
+
+Or work with "threshold-weighted collateral" per token, as Aave does. After this fix, add a separate borrow LTV (L-4). Otherwise users can borrow right up to 80%.
+
+### H-2 — wstETH is priced with the stETH/USD feed
+
+**Where:** `PriceOracle.getPrice` combined with the README's feed setup (`feeds[wstETH] = stETH/USD`). Used by `LendingMarket.collateralValueUsd` and `_liquidate`.
+
+One wstETH is worth `stEthPerToken` stETH, currently **1.2446** (Chainlink wstETH-stETH Exchange Rate feed on Arbitrum). The oracle only has one feed per token and reads stETH/USD as the wstETH price. That undervalues all wstETH collateral by ~19.7%, and the gap grows as staking rewards accrue.
+
+**What happens:**
+- Users can borrow ~20% less than they should. After the H-1 fix, they get liquidated at ~64% real LTV instead of 80%.
+- **Liquidators are overpaid.** `_liquidate` works out the collateral to seize as `seizeUsd / price` using the low price. For every $1 of debt repaid, the liquidator gets `1.05 × 1.2446 ≈ $1.307` of real wstETH. That's a ~31% bonus instead of 5%, taken from the borrower.
+- This also makes C-3 and H-1 more profitable to exploit.
+
+**Fix:** Price wstETH as `stETH/USD × wstETH-stETH rate`. Options:
+- Chainlink's wstETH/stETH exchange-rate feed (`0xB1552C5e96B312d0Bf8b554186F846C40614a540`, 18 dec) times stETH/USD.
+- A Chainlink wstETH/USD composite feed, if one is live on Arbitrum.
+
+Either way, the oracle needs a per-token adapter (base feed × rate source), not a single `feeds[token]` mapping. The same applies to weETH and ezETH (see "Before listing").
+
+### H-3 — Oracle does no validation (zero, negative, stale, sequencer down)
+
+**Where:** `PriceOracle.getPrice` (L39-45).
+
+It uses the deprecated `latestAnswer()` and trusts whatever comes back.
+
+| Input | Result |
+| --- | --- |
+| `answer == 0` on the **USDC** feed | `debtValueUsd == 0` → `healthFactor` returns `type(uint256).max` → **anyone can borrow the whole pool with no collateral** **[PoC]** |
+| `answer == 0` on a collateral feed | That collateral is worth 0 → every holder can be liquidated at once |
+| `answer < 0` | `uint256(answer)` becomes a huge number → the multiplication overflows → borrow, withdraw and liquidate all revert for affected users |
+| Stale answer (feed paused, heartbeat missed, feed deprecated) | Borrows against an old, higher price go through, and liquidations run at the wrong price |
+| Arbitrum sequencer down, then back up | Prices are frozen while it's down. When it restarts, liquidations fire on the jump before users can add collateral. Users with L1 force-inclusion can also act on stale prices while it's down |
+| `feed.decimals() > 18` | Underflow → revert (DoS only; not true for today's feeds) |
+
+**Fix:** In `getPrice`:
+- Use `latestRoundData()` and require `answer > 0`, `updatedAt != 0`, and `block.timestamp - updatedAt <= heartbeat[token]`. Look up each feed's heartbeat on data.chain.link and add a small buffer.
+- Check the Arbitrum **L2 Sequencer Uptime Feed** (`0xFdB631F5EE196F0ed6FAa767959853A9F217697D`, confirmed on-chain). Revert if it's down, and add a grace period (e.g. 1 hour) after it comes back before liquidations are allowed.
+- Consider min/max price bounds per feed.
+- In `healthFactor`, return `max` **before** calling the oracle when debt is 0. That way users with no debt can always withdraw, even while a feed is failing.
+
+### H-4 — `borrowWithSig` signatures can be replayed forever
+
+**Where:** `LendingMarket.borrowWithSig` (L162-168). Type hash `Borrow(address borrower,uint256 amount)`.
+
+The signed message has no nonce and no deadline, and the contract doesn't record used signatures. Anyone who sees one signature can send it again as many times as they like.
+
+**What the attacker gets:** The borrowed USDC goes to the victim, so there's no direct theft. But the attacker can replay the signature until the victim is borrowed right up to the limit **[PoC: one 1,000 USDC signature replayed 5×, 5,000 USDC debt]**. Then they wait for any small price move (H-1 makes this almost instant) and liquidate the victim for the bonus. The victim also pays interest on debt they never asked for. The signature also never expires, so a signature leaked years later still works.
+
+**Fix:** Change the struct to `Borrow(address borrower,uint256 amount,address receiver,uint256 nonce,uint256 deadline)`:
+- Keep `nonces[borrower]++` on each use and require `block.timestamp <= deadline`.
+- Consider binding `msg.sender` (the relayer) or `receiver`.
+- Use OZ `ECDSA.recover` (rejects malleable signatures and `address(0)`) or `SignatureChecker` (also supports ERC-1271 smart-contract wallets).
+
