+
+## Medium
+
+### M-1 — Reentrancy in `CollateralVault.withdraw` (sends tokens before updating state)
+
+**Where:** `CollateralVault.withdraw` (L44-53).
+
+```solidity
+IERC20(token).transfer(msg.sender, amount);   // external call first
+balanceOf[msg.sender][token] = balance - amount; // uses the old `balance`
+```
+
+If a listed token calls back into the receiver on transfer (ERC-777, some "yield" tokens, or any token with transfer hooks), the receiver can call `withdraw` again while its balance is still the old value. Each nested call pays out again. When the calls unwind, the balance is set to the same `balance - amount`, so only one withdrawal gets recorded. The health check at the end reads the balance before the outer write, so it doesn't catch this either.
+
+**What the attacker gets:** Deposit X, withdraw X many times over. That drains every other user's balance of that token from the vault. wstETH has no hooks, so this is **not exploitable at launch**. It becomes Critical the day a hook-enabled token is listed, and the roadmap is open-ended.
+
+**Fix:** Update the balance before the transfer (checks-effects-interactions: check, then update state, then call out). Add `nonReentrant` to `deposit`, `withdraw` and `seize`, and to the market's entry points. Call `market.requireHealthy` after the state change but before the transfer, or keep it last with a reentrancy guard in place.
+
+### M-2 — Transfer return values ignored; deposits credited at the requested amount
+
+**Where:**
+- `CollateralVault.deposit` (L38-39), `withdraw` (L48), `seize` (L57).
+- `LendingMarket.repay` (L177), `_liquidate` (L213), `_borrow` (L204).
+
+1. **Unchecked `bool`.** For a token that returns `false` instead of reverting, `deposit` credits collateral that never arrived. That's unlimited borrowing against nothing. USDC, wstETH, weETH and ezETH all revert on failure, so this isn't live today. But `listCollateral` is a general-purpose listing path.
+2. **Fee-on-transfer tokens.** The vault credits `amount`, not what actually arrived. So its recorded balances add up to more than it holds, and the last users to withdraw can't. USDT has a fee switch in its code; check the Arbitrum USDT0 token before listing.
+3. **Rebasing tokens** (the DAO's "rebasing collateral" request, e.g. stETH itself). A negative rebase leaves the vault short, again hitting the last users to withdraw. A positive rebase leaves extra tokens in the vault that nobody is credited with and nobody can withdraw.
+
+**Fix:** Use OZ `SafeERC20` everywhere. In `deposit`, credit `balanceAfter - balanceBefore`. Keep a written policy: **list only non-rebasing wrappers** (wstETH, not stETH), or build share-based accounting before listing any rebasing token.
+
+### M-3 — `borrowers` grows without limit → `liquidateAll` stops working
+
+**Where:** `LendingMarket._borrow` (L200), `LendingMarket.liquidateAll` (L190-195).
+
+`borrowers.push(borrower)` runs whenever `principalOf[borrower] == 0`, and entries are never removed. `borrow(0)` costs almost nothing on Arbitrum and adds an entry every time **[PoC: 50 duplicate entries from 50 `borrow(0)` calls]**. So does any borrow small enough to round to 0 principal (L-2), and every repay-then-borrow cycle. `liquidateAll` loops over the whole array. For each entry it calls `healthFactor`, which loops over every collateral token with external oracle calls. With enough junk entries, it runs out of gas.
+
+A single failing liquidation (e.g. a collateral token that's paused) also reverts the whole sweep. The keeper has to pay off the sum of all debts in one transaction too.
+
+**What happens:** The README says the keeper relies on `liquidateAll` "after a large price move", which is exactly when timing matters most. An attacker can cheaply block it, so liquidations are late and bad debt builds up. Per-user `liquidate` still works, so this is Medium, not High.
+
+**Fix:** Remove `liquidateAll`. Have the keeper work out which positions to liquidate off-chain and call `liquidate(user)` for each, or a batched `liquidate(address[] users)` with a try/catch for each user. Reject `amount == 0`. If you still need an on-chain list, use a set (e.g. `EnumerableSet`) and remove users when their debt reaches zero.
+
+### M-4 — Full liquidation only, and no bad-debt path
+
+**Where:** `LendingMarket._liquidate` (L209-234).
+
+The liquidator must repay **the whole debt** and gets up to `debt × 1.05` in collateral. Once a position's collateral is worth less than `debt × 1.05` (after a fast price drop or an oracle gap), liquidating it loses money. Nobody will do it. The debt stays open, still accruing interest, backed by nothing. The loop also quietly stops when collateral runs out, so a liquidator who does step in pays the full debt and gets less back than they expected.
+
+Full-close liquidation also means one liquidator needs cash equal to the largest position. That makes large positions slower to liquidate.
+
+**What happens:** The treasury's USDC takes the loss, and nothing tracks it. There's no view of protocol solvency.
+
+**Fix:**
+- Add a close factor (e.g. up to 50% of debt per call, with full close allowed below a small debt size or a health factor below ~0.95).
+- Let the liquidator choose `repayAmount`.
+- Cap the seized amount at what the user has, and cut `repayAmount` to match.
+- When collateral runs out and debt remains, write it off explicitly (`badDebt += remaining; principalOf[user] = 0`) so it's visible and gets covered by the treasury.
+
+### M-5 — The treasury can't withdraw liquidity or interest
+
+**Where:** `LendingMarket` (missing function).
+
+The README says the treasury seeds USDC "with a direct transfer and takes the interest". But the contract has no function that moves USDC out, except `_borrow` to a borrower. Seeded liquidity and all interest earned stay locked. Today the only way out is C-1 (anyone upgrades and takes it). Once C-1 is fixed, the only way out is an owner upgrade.
+
+**Fix:** Add an `onlyOwner` `withdrawReserves(to, amount)`, limited to the USDC that isn't lent out. Track `totalBorrows` so the treasury can't pull liquidity that borrowers need to repay into. Emit an event.
+
