+
+## Low
+
+### L-1 — Interest uses `block.number × 12`; on Arbitrum that's an approximate L1 block number
+
+**Where:** `LendingMarket.accrueInterest` (L118-121), `SECONDS_PER_BLOCK = 12`.
+
+On Arbitrum, `block.number` returns an *approximate L1* block number. It's shared across many L2 blocks and can jump. Arbitrum's docs say it's only reliable over hours, not minutes. Over the long run it follows L1 at 12s per slot, so accrual is roughly right today. But it's rough over short periods, and it silently breaks if Ethereum changes its slot time (shorter slots are on the roadmap). Halving the slot time halves every borrower's interest. The README also says interest follows "elapsed time".
+
+**Fix:** Use `block.timestamp` and a `lastAccrualTime` variable. Since the market sits behind a proxy with append-only storage, add the new variable at the **end** and stop using `lastAccrualBlock`.
+
+### L-2 — Rounding favours users; leftover dust debt can't be cleared
+
+**Where:** `LendingMarket._borrow` (L201), `LendingMarket.repay` (L173-178).
+
+- `_borrow` rounds principal **down**. Once `borrowIndex > 1e18`, borrowing 1 wei of USDC adds 0 principal. It's also treated as a new borrower and pushed onto the list again (M-3).
+- `repay` sets the amount to `debtOf` (rounded down) and takes off `amount × WAD / borrowIndex` (also rounded down). A "repay everything" call leaves 1 wei of principal behind. Calling `repay` again takes off 0, forever **[PoC]**. While that dust exists, `withdraw` of all collateral reverts with `Unhealthy`, because the health factor is 0 when there's no collateral **[PoC]**. The user has to leave a small amount of collateral in the vault for good.
+
+**Fix:** Round in the protocol's favour: principal on borrow and debt displayed round **up**, principal taken off on repay rounds **down**. Also add a full-repay path: if `amount >= debt`, set `principalOf = 0` and pull the rounded-up debt. Reject `amount == 0`.
+
+### L-3 — `withdraw` checks health against an out-of-date borrow index
+
+**Where:** `CollateralVault.withdraw` → `LendingMarket.requireHealthy` (view).
+
+`requireHealthy` doesn't accrue interest. If nobody has used the market for a while, `borrowIndex` is behind, so users look healthier than they are. They can then withdraw slightly more than they should, leaving a position that can be liquidated right away.
+
+**Fix:** Make the health check use the up-to-date index. Either work out the current index in a view, or have the vault call a non-view `market.accrueAndRequireHealthy(user)`.
+
+### L-4 — No max LTV separate from the liquidation threshold
+
+**Where:** `LendingMarket._borrow` (L203).
+
+Borrowing is allowed anywhere up to health factor = 1.0, the same line used for liquidation. Once H-1 is fixed, a user who borrows the maximum is liquidatable after the next accrual or the smallest price tick. They lose the full liquidation bonus (M-4: full close). There's also no minimum position size, so tiny positions nobody bothers to liquidate can build up.
+
+**Fix:** Add a per-collateral `maxLtv` (e.g. 75% for wstETH vs 80% liquidation) for `_borrow` and `withdraw`. Add a minimum debt size.
+
+### L-5 — Initialization and upgrade safety
+
+**Where:** `LendingMarket.initialize` (L56), `LendingMarket.upgradeTo`, `MarketProxy.constructor`.
+
+- The implementation contract itself is never initialized, so anyone can call `initialize` on it and become its owner. There's little impact today: no `selfdestruct` and no delegatecall. But it's a standard trap.
+- If the proxy is deployed with empty `initData` and initialized in a separate transaction, someone can initialize first and become owner. After C-1 is fixed, that owner can upgrade the proxy and take everything. Arbitrum's first-come-first-served sequencer makes this harder, not impossible.
+- There's a circular dependency: `CollateralVault.market` is immutable and must be the **proxy** address, and `initialize` needs the vault. The deploy script has to predict the proxy address (CREATE nonce or CREATE2) and pass `initData` to the proxy constructor. The PoC did this and it works, but there's no deploy script in the repo yet.
+- The "append-only storage" rule is enforced only by the README.
+
+**Fix:** Add a constructor to `LendingMarket` that sets `initialized = true` (or OZ `_disableInitializers()`). Always deploy the proxy with `initData`. Add the upgrade checks from C-1. Add `forge inspect LendingMarket storageLayout` diffing to CI, or use OZ upgrades-core validation.
+
+### L-6 — `listCollateral` doesn't check the feed; no delisting; no cap on the list
+
+**Where:** `LendingMarket.listCollateral` (L107-114), `CollateralVault.setSupported`.
+
+- A token listed before `setFeed` is called can be deposited. Every borrower holding it then gets `FeedNotSet` reverts from `healthFactor`, which means they can't borrow, withdraw or be liquidated.
+- There's no way to stop new deposits of a token that's become risky.
+- `collateralTokens` has no size cap, and every health check loops over all of it with 3+ external calls per token held.
+
+**Fix:** Require `oracle.getPrice(token)` to succeed when listing. Add a delist or pause-deposits function (`vault.setSupported(token, false)` exists but is never reachable). Cap the number of collateral tokens.
+
+### L-7 — Admin controls: one-step handover, no bounds, no timelock
+
+**Where:** `PriceOracle.transferOwnership` (L28), `LendingMarket.setBorrowRate` (L101), and all setters once C-1 to C-3 are fixed.
+
+- `PriceOracle.transferOwnership` takes effect in one step and accepts `address(0)`. A typo loses control of pricing for good.
+- The oracle owner is the deployer EOA and is separate from the market owner. Whoever holds that key controls every price, which is the same power as C-2.
+- `setBorrowRate` has no cap. A compromised or careless owner can set a rate that makes every position liquidatable within hours.
+- `LendingMarket` has no ownership transfer at all (I-2).
+
+**Fix:** Use OZ `Ownable2Step` on both contracts. Put the owner behind a multisig and timelock (e.g. 24-48h) for oracle, threshold, rate and upgrade changes. Add bounds: `borrowRate <= 10_000`, and threshold/bonus as in C-3. Have a separate guardian that can only pause.
+
+### L-8 — Signature details
+
+**Where:** `LendingMarket.borrowWithSig`, `initialize` (domain separator).
+
+- `domainSeparator` is computed once at `initialize` with that `block.chainid`. After a chain fork, signatures are valid on both chains. Rebuild it when `block.chainid` differs from the cached value (the OZ `EIP712` pattern).
+- Raw `ecrecover` accepts high-`s` (malleable) signatures. For `borrower == address(0)` it treats an invalid signature as valid, though `_borrow` reverts later on the health check.
+- Smart-contract wallets (Safe) can't use this function.
+
+**Fix:** OZ `EIP712` + `SignatureChecker`, together with the H-4 fix.
+
